\description{
Functional bootstrapping refreshes a ciphertext and applies a
look-up table to the encrypted integers in the same step. The input
is an RLWE ciphertext converted to CKKS with
\code{\link[=schemelet_convert_rlwe_to_ckks]{schemelet_convert_rlwe_to_ckks()}}, and the output goes back with
\code{\link[=schemelet_convert_ckks_to_rlwe]{schemelet_convert_ckks_to_rlwe()}}.
}
\details{
\code{eval_fbt_setup()} prepares the context; call
\code{\link[=eval_bootstrap_key_gen]{eval_bootstrap_key_gen()}} and \code{\link[=eval_mult_key_gen]{eval_mult_key_gen()}} after it.
\code{eval_fbt()} applies the table. \code{eval_fbt_no_decoding()} stops
before the return to coefficient encoding, leaving a slot-packed
CKKS ciphertext on which leveled operations can be done;
\code{eval_hom_decoding()} then finishes.

The R type of \code{coeffs} selects the form of the table, as the Python
type does in openfhe-python. An integer vector is the optimized
Boolean case (input modulus 2, order 1), with the two coefficients
\code{c(f(1), f(0) - f(1))}; build it with \code{as.integer()}. A double or
complex vector is the general case, with the coefficients from
\code{\link[=get_hermite_trig_coefficients]{get_hermite_trig_coefficients()}}.

\code{p_in}, \code{p_out}, \code{big_q}, \code{initial_scaling} and
\code{post_scaling} are strings of decimal digits, or whole numbers of
at most 2^53.
